        "question_title": "Three pieces of a copper–tin alloy have masses in the ratio \\(3:4:5\\). The third piece contains \\(40\\% \\) copper. The copper percentage in the second piece is \\(a\\) times that in the third piece, while that in the first piece is \\(1/a\\) times that in the third piece, where \\(a>1\\). If the overall alloy contains at least \\(50\\% \\) copper and no piece contains more than \\(100\\% \\) copper, then \\(a\\) lies in which interval?",
        "question_instruction": "Choose one correct option.",
        "explanation": "The overall copper percentage is \\n\\(\\frac{3(40/a)+4(40a)+5(40)}{12}\\ge50\\). Thus \\(4a+\\frac{3}{a}+5\\ge15\\), so \\(4a^2-10a+3\\ge0\\). Its roots are \\(\\frac{5\\pm\\sqrt{13}}{4}\\). Since \\(a>1\\), we obtain \\(a\\ge\\frac{5+\\sqrt{13}}{4}\\). The second piece has copper percentage \\(40a\\le100\\), giving \\(a\\le\\frac{5}{2}\\).",
